According to Gauss's law, the total electric flux through a closed surface is given by the net charge enclosed divided by the permittivity of free space.
\phi = \frac{q_{\text{en}}}{\epsilon_0}
If a charge q is placed at the center of one surface of a cube, it is effectively shared by two identical cubes. To completely enclose the charge, we can imagine placing another identical cube adjacent to the first one, such that the charge is at the center of the common face. In this configuration, the total flux through the combined system of two cubes would be \(q/\epsilon_0\).
Since the charge is symmetrically placed on the common face, the flux linked with each cube is half of the total flux.
q_{\text{en}} = \frac{q}{2}
Therefore, the flux linked with the given cube is:
\phi = \frac{q}{2\epsilon_0}

